---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout title="News · Space Array" active="news.html" footer="© 2026 Space Array Initiative">

<main>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="kicker">Latest updates</div>
    <h1>News</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Follow the latest developments of the Space Array Initiative,
      including mission studies, workshops, scientific activities,
      community events and upcoming talks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">

    <article class="card" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
      <div class="kicker">November 2026</div>
      <h2>Mm/Sub-mm/FIR Space Interferometry: Present and Future</h2>

      <p>
        15 to 19 November 2027, STFC Rutherford Appleton Laboratory,
        Harwell Campus, Didcot, UK.
      </p>

      <p>
        The meeting will bring the international mm/sub-mm/FIR
        space-interferometry community together to discuss recent
        scientific results, ongoing technology developments, and future
        directions in the field. Topics include the imaging of the photon
        rings of black holes, extragalactic masers, and high angular
        resolution far-infrared astronomy.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="https://indico.stfc.ac.uk/event/1959/" target="_blank">Event page and call for abstracts</a>
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="card" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
      <div class="kicker">10 June 2026</div>
      <h2>Space Array website launched</h2>

      <p>
        The initial Space Array website is now online. The site presents
        the long-term vision for a distributed space interferometer,
        introduces the SHARP mission concept, and provides information
        about the team and ongoing activities.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- <article class="card" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
      <div class="kicker">June 2026</div>
      <h2>SHARP mission concept featured on the website</h2>

      <p>
        A dedicated page describing the SHARP mission concept has been
        added to the website. SHARP explores the scientific and technical
        foundations of future space-based submillimeter interferometry.
      </p>
    </article> -->

  </section>

</main>

</Layout>
